// #120 CLOSED THIS ROW. It was `type_apply_trap.ledger`'s `apply_nongeneric`:
// "compile 0 / run 132 / demoted to trap stubs" — a program that COMPILED
// CLEAN and died on SIGILL. The ledger existed precisely because neither
// outcome fits the corpus harness, and it did its job: when #120 made a trap
// demotion in the FINAL emission round route through the malfunction sink, the
// gate noticed the row had IMPROVED and said so, and its own instruction is
// what this file is following — delete the row, land the behaviour as an
// ordinary test.
//
// `Marked` takes NO type parameters. `type_apply("Marked", [type_of::<i32>()])`
// applies one type argument to it. Nothing checks arity — not the parser, not
// sema, not mono — so mono demotes the caller to a trap stub. What changed is
// only what happens next: the demotion is now a refusal instead of an object
// file.
//
// ⚠ THE ARITY DEFECT ITSELF IS STILL OPEN and is not this file's subject: the
// right answer is a diagnostic naming `Marked` and its arity at the
// `type_apply` site, not a trap stub refused one layer down. What is pinned
// here is that the compiler REFUSES rather than shipping `ud2`.
//
// ⚠ THE COMPILER IS NOT SILENT, AND SAYING SO WOULD BE OVERSTATING IT. It
// prints, on stderr, with exit code 0:
//     mono: note: 2 unresolved struct template(s)/name(s) (awaiting metaprog
//     emission?); 1 fn(s) demoted to trap stubs
// A `note`, with no source location, naming no name, phrased as a wait rather
// than a refusal — and the trap stub it mentions IS the SIGILL. So the
// diagnostic exists and carries the wrong register: an unsatisfiable demand is
// reported in the same words as a demand that a later emission round will
// satisfy, and the exit code cannot tell them apart.
//
// It is the same missing check as `apply_nonliteral`, one argument to the
// right: `__type_apply__` validates the SHAPE of its first argument by aborting
// and does not validate the second at all.
package type_apply_nongeneric;

use logos.std.compiler.metaprog;

pub struct Marked { pub a: i32, }

unsafe fn main() -> i32 {
    let t = type_apply("Marked", [type_of::<i32>()]);
    if t.uid == (0 as u64) { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
